# Copy to .env at the repo root. `make` loads it for every target.
# These defaults line up with docker-compose.yml and are safe for local development only.

ENVIRONMENT=local

# docker-compose publishes Postgres 16 on 5433 to avoid a local install on 5432. If 5433 is
# already taken on your machine — another project's compose stack is the usual culprit, and
# the symptom is a container stuck in `Created` — change both of these to the same free port.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://rascal:rascal@localhost:5433/rascal

# Alembic only. In production this is Neon's *direct* endpoint while DATABASE_URL is the
# *pooled* one — a migration through a transaction-mode pooler fails confusingly. There is
# no pooler locally, so leave it unset and Alembic falls back to DATABASE_URL.
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=

# 32 bytes, because anything shorter trips PyJWT's InsecureKeyLengthWarning on every call.
# Generate the production value with `openssl rand -hex 32` — see docs/DEPLOY.md section 3.
JWT_SECRET=0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef

# Object storage — Tigris, which is S3-compatible. See docs/tigris-setup.md.
# Tests still stub this with moto and presigning is offline, so the placeholders below are
# enough to run the suite; a real upload needs real values.
S3_BUCKET=rascal-media-dev
# Tigris is globally distributed and has no AWS-style regions. Leaving a real region here
# produces a signature error that reads like a credentials problem.
S3_REGION=auto
# https://t3.storage.dev from a laptop or CI; https://fly.storage.tigris.dev from inside Fly.
# Unset means real AWS, which is how this ran before Tigris.
S3_ENDPOINT_URL=https://t3.storage.dev
# Tigris keys look like tid_… / tsec_…. `tigris access-keys create` prints the secret once.
# A new key has NO permissions until you run:
#   tigris access-keys assign <id> --bucket <bucket> --role Editor
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=local-dev-access-key
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=local-dev-secret-key

# The `aud` claim every Apple identity token is checked against — an allowlist, comma-separated.
# One value is the normal case and is what production sets. A second is for a client that is a
# different audience for the same Apple credential, e.g. a browser signing in through a Services
# ID. A token whose `aud` is on the list passes; anything else is still rejected.
#
# Setting more than one value makes APNS_TOPIC required — see below.
APPLE_BUNDLE_ID=com.travisterry.childarchive

# --- Google sign-in --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated OAuth client ids, checked against a Google identity token's `aud`.
#
# **A separate list from APPLE_BUNDLE_ID and never the same values.** `aud` is a claim name the
# two providers share over entirely different namespaces; pooling them would let an Apple token
# for a Google client id verify.
#
# **Not a secret.** An iOS OAuth client is a public client — Google issues no client secret for
# it, which is why the flow uses PKCE — and this string ships inside every copy of the app.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS=22574619282-vs702oo01a8vne7tsbc6h3ilk6a0sje6.apps.googleusercontent.com

# --- Mail ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `console` (default) writes the whole message to stdout and never opens a socket, which is
# what makes it impossible for a local run to send real mail to a real person. `resend` is the
# only other value and needs MAIL_API_KEY.
#
# This said `postmark` until Aug 17, 2026, which had been wrong since phase 60: D8 replaced
# Postmark with Resend and the adapter was deleted rather than disabled, so the name here named
# nothing. A stale example file is the one place a wrong value gets copied into a real .env.
# MAIL_PROVIDER=console
# MAIL_API_KEY=
MAIL_FROM=Vellum <hello@rascal.local>
# Where replies go, if that is not MAIL_FROM. Unset means replies go to MAIL_FROM.
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# --- The marketing signup (phase 60) ----------------------------------------------------------
# These three were missing from this file entirely, so a developer copying it got a
# `POST /v1/subscribe` that answers 503 with nothing here to suggest why.
#
# **Turnstile unset means the route refuses, and that is the design rather than an oversight** —
# no rate limit applies to this endpoint by the owner's call, so this is the whole protection.
# There is deliberately no local bypass: a key that skips verification when unset is the defect
# the phase was written to avoid.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
# The Resend audience the signup forwards into. There is no `subscribers` table — the list is
# provider-held — so this id is where a subscriber actually lives. Unset means the route refuses
# rather than accepting an address it cannot store.
# RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=
# Browser origins allowed to call the subscribe route, comma-separated. Never `*`. Defaults to
# the GitHub Pages origin; a custom domain is a second entry here rather than a replacement.
# MARKETING_ORIGINS=https://travist6983.github.io

# --- Double opt-in (phase 88) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the marketing site lives. Two consumers: the confirmation link sends the browser back
# here after a click, and the daily broadcast's footer links here. It replaced a hardcoded
# github.io URL in services/broadcast.py that went stale the day the custom domain landed.
# MARKETING_SITE_URL=https://getvellumapp.com
# Where the emailed confirmation link points. Its own setting rather than derived, for the same
# reason EMAIL_SIGN_IN_LINK_BASE is: the host mail links to and the host the API answers on are
# the same today and are not the same thing.
# SUBSCRIBE_CONFIRM_LINK_BASE=https://rascal-api.fly.dev/v1/subscribe/confirm
# Short on purpose, and not for secrecy — the token is signed, so age does not weaken it. A
# confirmation link stays good for its whole life, so this is what bounds a stale link reopened
# after an unsubscribe. See services/subscribe_token.py.
# SUBSCRIBE_CONFIRM_TTL_DAYS=7

# --- The daily prompt broadcast (phase 69) ----------------------------------------------------
# **The switch that lets marketing email actually send, and it defaults to off.**
#
# Deliberately separate from the credentials above: MAIL_API_KEY and RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID exist to
# make POST /v1/subscribe work, and setting them must not also start mailing the list. Nobody
# setting a secret expects that.
#
# Off, `python -m rascal.jobs.broadcast` does everything except the network call -- picks the
# prompt, renders both bodies, writes its ledger row as `skipped`, and logs what it would have
# sent. Open item 12: no marketing email before the P0 behavioural gate.
# MARKETING_EMAIL_ENABLED=false
# The hour the daily prompt goes out, in BROADCAST_TIMEZONE. 8am, because a prompt that asks a
# parent to notice something today has already missed it by 8pm. The Fly machine runs HOURLY and
# this decides which run acts -- `jobs/notify.py` established that shape: hourly is the floor Fly
# gives, and a `--schedule daily` machine fires roughly whenever it was created. A run outside
# the hour writes no ledger row, because a row claims the day.
# BROADCAST_SEND_HOUR=8
# BROADCAST_TIMEZONE=America/Detroit

# --- Push notifications (APNs) -------------------------------------------------------------
# All optional, and unset is the state this repository ships in: no APNs key exists yet, and
# it comes from the same Apple Developer enrollment as Sign in with Apple. See
# docs/blocked.md section 1a and docs/runbook-deploy.md section 4a.
#
# Unset means `build_push_client` returns a client that sends nothing and says so loudly at
# WARNING, rather than one that quietly reports success. All three are required together; a
# partial set is treated as unconfigured, because "nearly working" hides which piece is
# missing. Nothing here is needed to run the suite — it uses a stub.
#
# APNS_PRIVATE_KEY is the *contents* of the .p8, not a path to it.
# APNS_KEY_ID=
# APNS_TEAM_ID=
# APNS_PRIVATE_KEY=
# The APNs topic is one bundle id, never a list. It defaults to APPLE_BUNDLE_ID while that
# names exactly one audience. Set it if the two ever legitimately differ — a wrong value
# rejects every token with DeviceTokenNotForTopic.
#
# **Required once APPLE_BUNDLE_ID names more than one audience**, and the app refuses to start
# without it rather than picking the first one: silently guessing would be right today and
# wrong the day somebody reorders a comma-separated secret, which is a failure whose only
# symptom is that no push is ever delivered.
# APNS_TOPIC=
